WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.088
Here's Oracle on the fixtures.

2
00:00:02.088 --> 00:00:04.937
Day one gives four notices, one per chain.

3
00:00:04.937 --> 00:00:08.126
The maintenance names a
database system and its window.

4
00:00:08.126 --> 00:00:11.159
The change is action
required, by December 15,

5
00:00:11.159 --> 00:00:16.488
because Oracle's own action-required fields
say so, and it names a load balancer.

6
00:00:16.488 --> 00:00:20.075
A week later, the provider has
rescheduled the maintenance.

7
00:00:20.075 --> 00:00:24.534
Because the kit keeps only the latest
in each chain, the new announcement

8
00:00:24.534 --> 00:00:29.438
replaces the original, with the new window,
rather than becoming a second notice.

9
00:00:29.438 --> 00:00:32.650
That's exactly what the guide's
try-it says to look for.

10
00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:36.188
Beyond monitoring the provider
and receiving advisories,

11
00:00:36.188 --> 00:00:40.882
this gate supports contingency planning:
maintenance that names the agency's

12
00:00:40.882 --> 00:00:43.626
resources prompts a
contingency plan review,

13
00:00:43.626 --> 00:00:45.937
and the task carries a flag that says so.

14
00:00:45.937 --> 00:00:50.290
Until the reader exists, Oracle's
announcement email is the interim path,

15
00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:52.970
and the settings file
names who receives it.
